15 0 obj
<< /A 40 0 R /Alt (Two line graphs labeled a and b show temperature in degrees Celsius on the y-axis versus days on the x-axis, each comparing five classes distinguished by color with sample sizes shown in the legends. Both plots display varied temperature trends for the classes over twenty-one days, with class-specific patterns such as a rapid initial decrease, increase, or stabilization near thirty-seven degrees Celsius.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 41 0 R /Alt (Two Kaplan-Meier line graphs labeled a and b display survival probability over 28 days for five classes, with survival decreasing more sharply for Class 3. Both graphs include p-values less than 0.001 indicating significant differences between groups, and tables below show the number of subjects at risk per class at specified time points.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 42 0 R /Alt (Four line charts labeled a, b, c, and d display temperature trends in degrees Celsius and predicted mortality risk for five classes distinguished by color. Each chart shows temperature variation over a short time on the left, separated by a dashed vertical line from a longer-term predicted mortality risk up to twenty-eight days on the right. The x-axes represent days, and the y-axes present temperature and predicted mortality risk percentage. Each class exhibits distinct temperature and risk patterns across all panels.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (Four-panel scientific figure showing temperature trajectories and corresponding predicted mortality risk for five classes, with panels a, b, c, and d representing increasing time intervals for observation. Left y-axis shows temperature in degrees Celsius, right y-axis shows predicted mortality risk in percent, and x-axis displays days. Each class is color-coded and exhibits distinct temperature patterns in the initial days before converging toward predicted mortality risk curves after observation period marked by a dashed vertical line. Legend identifies class colors.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Panel a shows a line graph with hazard ratio for in-hospital mortality on the y-axis and temperature on the x-axis, indicating a U-shaped relationship where risks are high at temperature extremes. Panel b presents three colored curves comparing hazard ratio versus temperature across different days, all showing increased mortality risk at lower and higher temperatures. Panel c displays a line graph of hazard ratio for mortality over days, indicating an initial peak followed by stabilization. Panel d visualizes hazard ratio versus days for three temperatures, with the highest temperature showing elevated and sustained mortality risk over time.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 45 0 R /Alt (Figure with four panels \(a\205d\) showing hazard ratios \(HR\) for in-hospital mortality. Panel a: Line graph with confidence shading showing HR versus temperature, with HR lowest between 34 and 38 degrees Celsius. Panel b: Line graphs for three different days \(two, five, and twelve\) showing HR versus temperature, all U-shaped, with minimum risk near 36 to 38 degrees Celsius. Panel c: Line graph with confidence shading showing HR versus days, initially rising then stabilizing. Panel d: Three lines for different baseline temperatures \(thirty-six, thirty-eight, and forty degrees Celsius\) showing HR versus days; HR highest and increasing over time for forty degrees Celsius.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
